    "episode_id": 96781,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96781.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 73,
    "turn_text": "That's good. Thank you for the calls. I appreciate that. And again, the chief of department, the highest ranking uniformed officer in the New York City Police Department, he very interestingly criticized the officers and said, maybe you're in the wrong line of work. And here's why. And it's something that you and I said yesterday. And it is that, one, first of all, you don't know it was just water. It could be a caustic chemical, right? It could be gasoline. It could be human bodily fluids, right? It could be a number of things. But the deal is if the targeting and taunting and attacking of police officers is at this level, who knows what level it will be at tomorrow? And the deal is that if you don't stand up to it, you invite it. It's unfortunate the world is that way, but we know the world is that way. I tried to reach Chief Buckner today to get his thoughts on it. I wasn't able to reach him. There's a big conference of police chiefs from across the state, so he's probably a way at that. But I was able to speak to I swapped messages with the Chief at Chitnango, and I was able to speak with the Chief at a place called Brighton in suburban Monroe County, and I was able to speak with the Chief of the Rochester Police Department. And the Chief from Rochester in Brighton, they each said, that they would totally support their officers making an arrest in that situation. And that there are a variety of things for which the individuals throwing the water could be arrested, interfering with governmental administration and harassment. And also another issue is even beyond the indignity of it, those officers are wearing a fair amount of kind of expensive taxpayer funded electrical equipment. The camera, the taser, the radio and I don't know I presume those are somewhat water resistant or whatever else like that but getting them drenched like that certainly can't be very good for them. And here's what Fred Corey, the chit-nango police chief, said in a text. He said, I can say it is very disheartening the way law enforcement is viewed in today's day and age. Society is in a downward trend, and unless we get back to the basic tenets of respecting law and government and the people who apply it and serve, nothing good will come from these actions. Our leaders need to stand behind their police and give them the backing they deserve. I agree with that 100%. And I think that actions like this disrespect antagonistic, aggressive, quasi-violent attacks like this, I think that they are driven. Excuse me, bye. some of our politicians. I think that I think that they're driven by some of our pastors. I think that they're driven by some of our activists. When you curse the police all day, every day, you've got to understand that that affects the way people will interact with and perceive those police officers. And I would also say in Syracuse to Mayor Ben, that after this last matter with the video camera, and the city's position was so obsequious where it was, you know, sorry, sorry, bend over, grab our ankles. That tone is not seen as respect, you showing respect. It is seen as you showing weakness. And it does not improve relations, as you hope. It rather invites derision, which unfortunately manifests itself at best in some offensive videos and at worst in some injured or worse police officers. You know what's great about your investment account with the big guys? It's actually a time machine. Log in and zoom. Welcome back to 1999.",
