    "turn_idx": 84,
    "turn_text": "not an insignificant thing. Destiny has a right, just like anybody else, to raise their voice and be heard as policies and decisions are made. And the fact that the thing which the mall desires is not the thing which the editorial board of the newspaper or the progressive establishment of this region wants, doesn't mean that the mall doesn't have the right to raise its voice, doesn't mean that you and I don't have the right to raise our voices. Christopher Communities has proposed south of Gorland Avenue, the construction in partnership with Elm Crest Children's Center of 50, two and three bedroom apartments, which will be for young single mothers between the ages of 18 and 25, all will be subsidized. This is of course on the east side of Syracuse. My quick observation here would be that providing these services is a nice thing, right? It would be better if we attacked the issue of, you know, single parent birth. I was born to a single parent deal. Didn't work out very well. But there are 366 such families. The proposal is to put in all of Anandaga County. The proposal is to put one-six of them into this small apartment complex. It'll be 17 buildings. Again adjacent to Elmcrest Children's Center. The downside, of course, is that neighborhood is otherwise zoned for single family residents, meaning that everybody else who lives in that neighborhood, it's one family and one house with a very, very high percentage of owner-occupied housing. Now, you've got conflicting interests here. You bring in this very large concentration of the county's young, single mothers in poverty with special needs children and the mothers with special needs themselves, you concentrate them in one neighborhood. I think that that may hurt the prospects of rehabilitation or progress or whatever you're looking for on the part of the mothers and children involved. And I think that it has a thunderclap impact on that neighborhood because going from single-family house zoning to high-density subsidized special needs housing is just an interesting change. And if neighbors say, hold it now, I think we ought to be a little more sophisticated in our response to them than saying that they are heartless or uncaring. Richard, glad you called. Welcome to 570WSIR.",
