    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96781.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 140,
    "turn_text": "Welcome back, friends. I'm grateful to the Congressman for his time. We didn't get to talk today about a proposal that could end Regents exams in the state of New York. I'll put that in the folder. We'll come back to it tomorrow. It's a huge thing. And maybe some of our young high school friends might think it's a good idea, but it is unfortunately just the latest step in what is undeniably the dumbing down of public education in the state of New York. Coincidentally, there's a story in The Atlantic today, which points out that there's a pretty good case to be made that we're also dumbing down college in not just the state of New York, but in the United States. Characteristics that lead to educational success have fallen off significantly among college students in America. At the same time, the graduation rates have gone up significantly among college students in America. So they are studying less and graduating more with higher grades. How do you think that works, right? So I just want to say in China, they're not dumbing down either their public schools or their colleges. In the United States, we are undeniably. How do you think that's going to work out? I need to run. God bless you. Keep your chin up. God bless the United States of America. News is next. And then you and I'll be back here tomorrow, afternoon, three to six on 570 WSIR.",
